= Unit 8 · Finite fields and applications

*Bridge into this unit.* A finite field is constrained simultaneously by its additive characteristic and by polynomial root counts. Frobenius links these viewpoints: it is a field homomorphism whose fixed elements and iterates describe subfields. Keep a field's size distinct from the order of any one multiplicative element.

== Lecture 15 · Existence and structure of finite fields
#focus[Construct fields of prime-power order and explain why their elements are roots of a single polynomial. Recall splitting fields, degree, and Lagrange.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall the Frobenius binomial identity in prime characteristic and the root bound over a field.


#definition[Characteristic][The characteristic of a nonzero ring is the least positive $n$ with $n dot 1=0$, or zero when no such $n$ exists. A field of positive characteristic has prime characteristic: if $n=a b$ with $1<a,b<n$, then $(a dot 1)(b dot 1)=0$ contradicts the absence of zero divisors.]

A finite field $K$ has characteristic $p$ and contains a copy of $FF_p=ZZ slash p ZZ$. As a finite-dimensional vector space over this copy, it has $p^n$ elements, one for each coefficient tuple in a basis. Thus the order of every finite field is a prime power.

#strategy[Construct all roots in a splitting field, prove they are closed under field operations, and count them using the derivative.]

#theorem[Fields of every prime-power order][For each prime $p$ and $n>=1$, a field of $q=p^n$ elements exists. It is the set of roots of $x^q-x$ inside a splitting field over $FF_p$.]
#proof[Take a finite splitting field by Lecture 14. In characteristic $p$, the binomial theorem gives $(a+b)^p=a^p+b^p$, because the intermediate binomial coefficients are divisible by $p$. Iterating gives the same identity for exponent $q$. Hence roots of $x^q-x$ are closed under addition, negation, and multiplication; if $a!=0$ is a root, then $(a^(-1))^q=(a^q)^(-1)=a^(-1)$. The roots contain zero and one, so they form a field. The formal derivative of $x^q-x$ is $-1$, so no root repeats: a repeated linear factor would divide the derivative. Since the polynomial splits and has degree $q$, this field has exactly $q$ elements.]

Conversely, in a field of $q$ elements every nonzero element satisfies $a^(q-1)=1$ by Lagrange in its multiplicative group. Thus all elements are roots of $x^q-x$, and the field is its splitting field over $FF_p$.

#theorem[Uniqueness up to isomorphism][Any two fields of $q$ elements are isomorphic over $FF_p$.]
#proof[More generally, an isomorphism of base fields extends to an isomorphism of splitting fields of corresponding polynomials. Adjoin one root at a time: its irreducible minimal polynomial maps to an irreducible polynomial with a root in the other splitting field. The quotient construction gives an extension of the current embedding. Continue through a generating list of roots. The image contains all roots of the target polynomial, since an embedding preserves distinctness and multiplicities, and the target is generated by those roots. Hence the embedding is surjective. Apply this to $x^q-x$.]

#example[A field with four elements][$FF_4=FF_2[t] slash (t^2+t+1)$. If $alpha=[t]$, then $alpha^2=alpha+1$ and $alpha^3=1$. The elements are $0,1,alpha,alpha+1$. There is no field of six elements because six is not a prime power.]

#example[A complete small-field multiplication check][In $FF_2[alpha] slash (alpha^2+alpha+1)$, the nonzero elements are $1,alpha,alpha+1$. The relation gives $alpha^2=alpha+1$, $alpha(alpha+1)=1$, and $(alpha+1)^2=alpha$. Thus every nonzero element has an inverse, while addition satisfies $a+a=0$ for all elements. This cannot be the ring $ZZ slash 4ZZ$, whose element $[2]$ is a nonzero zero divisor.]

The characteristic and additive structure give quick diagnostic checks for a claimed finite field. A field of size $p^n$ is an $n$-dimensional vector space over $FF_p$; its additive group is therefore a product of $n$ cyclic groups of order $p$, not generally a cyclic group of order $p^n$.

#checkpoint("8.1", [Why does $ZZ slash 4 ZZ$ fail to be the four-element field?], [Its characteristic is four and $[2]^2=[0]$ with $[2]!=[0]$. The four-element field instead has characteristic two.])
#takeaways[The notation $FF_q$ denotes a field determined up to isomorphism, not generally the residue ring $ZZ slash q ZZ$.]
