== Lecture 16 · Multiplicative structure and applications
#focus[Prove cyclicity of a finite field's multiplicative group, use interpolation, and connect algebraic constructions to concrete computations.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall finite cyclic group orders and that a nonzero polynomial cannot have too many distinct roots.


#theorem[Finite multiplicative subgroups are cyclic][Every finite subgroup $G$ of the multiplicative group of a field is cyclic. In particular $FF_q^times$ has a generator of order $q-1$.]
#strategy[Construct an element whose order is the least common multiple of all element orders. Then use the bound on the number of roots of a polynomial.]
#proof[Let $m$ be that least common multiple. For each prime power $ell^a$ dividing $m$ maximally, some element has order divisible by $ell^a$; raising it to the part of its order coprime to $ell$ gives an element of order $ell^a$. Elements commute, and the product of commuting elements of pairwise coprime orders has order the product: if a power of the product is one, isolate each factor and use coprimality to show each order divides the exponent. Thus $G$ contains an element of order $m$. Every element of $G$ is a root of $x^m-1$, so $|G|<=m$ by the root bound, while the constructed cyclic subgroup gives $m<=|G|$. Equality proves the claim.]

#example[Finding a generator][$FF_7^times$ has order six. The powers of $3$ are $3,2,6,4,5,1$, so $3$ generates it. To test a candidate in a cyclic group of known order $N$, it suffices to check $g^(N/ell)!=1$ for each prime divisor $ell$ of $N$: any proper divisor order would divide one of these exponents.]

#theorem[Lagrange interpolation][For distinct $a_1,dots,a_n$ in a field $K$ and prescribed $b_1,dots,b_n$, there is a unique polynomial $f$ of degree less than $n$ with $f(a_i)=b_i$.]
#proof[Define $L_i(x)=product_(j!=i)(x-a_j) slash (a_i-a_j)$. All denominators are nonzero, and $L_i(a_k)$ is one for $k=i$ and zero otherwise. Then $f=sum_i b_i L_i$ has the required values and degree bound. Two such polynomials have a difference of degree less than $n$ with $n$ roots, so the root bound forces equality.]

This result explains the basic redundancy in polynomial evaluation codes. A degree-less-than-$k$ polynomial is represented by its values at $n>=k$ distinct field points. Any $k$ correct coordinates determine it. A nonzero difference of two message polynomials has at most $k-1$ zeros, so two distinct codewords differ in at least $n-k+1$ positions. This is a mathematical distance statement; constructing a decoding algorithm is a further task.

#example[Interpolation over a finite field][Over $FF_5$, the line through $(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$ is $f(x)=1+2x$, because $2^(-1)=3$ and the slope $(0-1)/2=-3=2$. The field inverse, not ordinary real division, supplies the coefficient.]

The Frobenius map $a |-> a^p$ is an automorphism of $FF_(p^n)$: it preserves addition and multiplication and is injective, hence surjective on a finite set. Its fixed elements solve $x^p-x=0$, so they are exactly $FF_p$. Advanced Algebra develops this viewpoint further.

#example[Reading the coding bound as a uniqueness argument][Take degree-less-than-three messages over $FF_7$ and evaluate at six distinct points. There are $7^3$ messages, and two different messages disagree in at least $6-3+1=4$ positions. If a received word differed from two distinct codewords in at most one position each, those codewords would differ in at most two positions, contradicting the bound. Thus at most one codeword lies within one error of a received word.]

This proves uniqueness of correction at that radius, not a procedure for finding the message. The argument separates a structural distance theorem from an algorithmic decoding question and shows exactly how a polynomial root bound becomes information about finite data.

#checkpoint("8.2", [How many zeros can the difference of distinct degree-less-than-four messages have?], [At most three. Therefore evaluation at seven distinct points produces codewords differing in at least four positions.])

